<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grand Vizier</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> was the chief minister of the Ottoman sultan, head of the government and the imperial council. Holding the sultan's seal, the grand vizier managed administration and often commanded armies, and in some periods, such as under the Köprülü family, held the real power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The title Grand Vizier, in Ottoman Turkish Vezir-i Azam or Sadrazam, comes from Classical Persian, while its components derive from Arabic. The word vizier comes from wazīr, from a root meaning to bear a burden, which reflects the official's role in carrying the burdens of the state for the sovereign. The rank was formally established as a state office in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, where the term was used in the 8th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took the office from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> in the early 14th century, and in the first phase of the state vizier was the only title in use.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first Ottoman vizier to be called Grand Vizier was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candarl-halil-pasha-the-younger">Çandarlı Halil Pasha</a> the Elder, in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad I</a>. The title distinguished the holder of the sultan's seal from other viziers. He was the first holder with a military background, and the first of a political family that rivalled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-dynasty">Ottoman dynasty</a> itself. His grandson Çandarlı Halil Pasha the Younger opposed the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and was executed two days after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> won it. After this the post went almost exclusively to men of the kul system and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devshirme">devshirme</a>, who were easier for the sultan to control.</p><p>Power was centralised in the office in the Köprülü era. Köprülü Mehmed Pasha, appointed in 1656 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-iv">Mehmed IV</a>, sent the sultan away from the city on hunting trips, removed officers suspected of corruption and executed those who stayed. On his deathbed five years later he persuaded the sultan to appoint his son Köprülü Fazıl Ahmed Pasha. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanzimat">Tanzimat</a> of the 19th century, Grand Viziers came to resemble the prime ministers of Western monarchies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Grand Vizier held the imperial seal and could convene all other viziers. In conference they were called the Kubbealtı viziers, after the Kubbealtı in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topkapi-palace">Topkapı Palace</a>, and the Grand Vizier's offices stood at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sublime-porte">Sublime Porte</a>. In Ottoman legal theory the sultan was supposed to conduct state affairs only through him, but the arrangement was often circumvented. As the Ottomanist Colin Imber writes, the sultan often had closer contact with the pages of the privy chamber and other courtiers than with the Grand Vizier.</p><p>Elsewhere the office had its own course. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> holders included Bairam Khan, who led the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> to victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-battle-of-panipat">Second Battle of Panipat</a>. In Qajar Iran the chief minister was styled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadr">sadr</a>-e aʿzam, a title adopted in the Ottoman manner.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Grand vizier” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Ur-Nammu's conquest an ensi was a governor in Ur III terms; the empire's second official, the <b>Grand Vizier</b>, resided in the province.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The second siege of Vienna failed in September 1683, when a relief army of imperial German and Polish troops under Jan III Sobieski routed the forces of <b>Grand Vizier</b> Kara Mustafa Pasha.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Philip V brought French methods to Spain, Peter the Great’s Russia pursued mercantilism, and the Ottoman <b>Grand Vizier</b> Kemankeş Kara Mustafa Pasha followed some of its financial policies.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/mercantilism">Mercantilism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>The forces of Leopold I, Holy Roman Emperor, under Charles of Lorraine met the army of Sultan Mehmed IV under the <b>Grand Vizier</b> Sarı Süleyman Pasha at Mohács, in the battle also called Harsány Mountain.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-12">12 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Mamluk cavalry under Janbirdi al-Ghazali attacked the Ottomans of <b>Grand Vizier</b> Hadım Sinan Pasha as they crossed Gaza on their way to Egypt, but the charge was broken.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-28">28 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
